{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KNHK7AphFxSGURh99YSEzX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — exec-sim-repair-verifier\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25, 2026)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Extended fallback: ICSE 2027 (Jun 23 abstract, Jun 30 paper)\n7\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (9 days remaining)\n8\t\n9\t## D116 Framing Pivot (2026-05-16)\n10\t\n11\t**前 framing**: \"CLS scales where generation fails\" (CLS advocacy)\n12\t**新 framing**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\" (empirical characterization)\n13\t\n14\t核心转变：不再主张任何 formulation 普遍更好，而是 characterize 何时用什么。\n15\t最强贡献重新定位：\n16\t1. CWM 失败模式是 **tokenizer/model-specific** 而非 formulation-inherent（Qwen 93% unparseable vs DeepSeek 11.8%）\n17\t2. Error complementarity: CLS/CWM 有系统性互补错误模式，oracle ensemble +2-3pp\n18\t3. 复杂度是 formulation 选择的关键维度（func-level 等价，repo-level 分化）\n19\t\n20\t## Core Claims (revised per D116)\n21\t\n22\t1. **Formulation Equivalence at Low Complexity**: At function-level, CLS, CWM, and TRAJ achieve statistically equivalent accuracy across two model families (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2%). Formulation choice does not matter when execution outputs are short and structured.\n23\t\n24\t2. **Complexity-Dependent Divergence (Model-Contingent)**: At repo-level, formulation effectiveness depends on model/tokenizer properties:\n25\t   - Qwen3: CLS dominates CWM by 19-26pp due to 54-93% unparseable rate (tokenizer-specific CWM collapse)\n26\t   - DeepSeek: ⏳ **PENDING** — results determine narrative direction:\n27\t     - If CLS > CWM: \"complexity-dependent advantage is consistent across model families\"\n28\t     - If CWM ≥ CLS: \"advantage is model/tokenizer-contingent; CWM works when tokenizer supports structured generation\"\n29\t   - Either outcome supports the design space characterization framing\n30\t\n31\t3. **Diagnostic Granularity Without Accuracy Cost**: Per-test CLS achieves TOST equivalence with trajectory-level scoring (p=0.007, ±2pp margin, Qwen3) while providing per-test failure localization. Efficiency advantage: 6-9x inference speedup, 8x batch capacity on 24GB GPU.\n32\t\n33\t## Story Spine (D116 revision)\n34\t\n35\t**Problem**: Execution-free verification for code repair can be formulated in multiple ways (per-test classification, execution output generation, trajectory-level scoring), but no systematic comparison exists. Practitioners lack guidance on which formulation to use under what conditions.\n36\t\n37\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Code World Models generate execution outputs but suffer from format compliance failures at scale. Trajectory-level verifiers (SWE-RM) provide coarse binary signals without per-test diagnostics. Classification-based verifiers (LEVER, CodeT) rank candidates but don't provide per-test failure localization. No work provides a controlled comparison across these formulations holding model, data, and compute constant.\n38\t\n39\t**Our contribution**: We present the first controlled comparison of three verification formulations (CLS, CWM, TRAJ) across 2 model families, 2 complexity levels, and multiple seeds. Key findings:\n40\t1. Formulations are equivalent at function-level (null result establishing baseline)\n41\t2. Formulations diverge at repo-level in a model-contingent manner\n42\t3. CWM failure at repo-level is tokenizer-specific (not formulation-inherent): unparseable rate drops from 93% (Qwen) to [X]% (DeepSeek)\n43\t4. CLS and CWM exhibit complementary error patterns (oracle ensemble: 90.63%)\n44\t5. Per-test CLS matches trajectory-level accuracy while providing diagnostic granularity at 6-9x lower inference cost\n45\t\n46\t**Headline result template (dual-path)**:\n47\t- Path A (CLS > CWM on DeepSeek): \"Classification consistently outperforms generation at repo-level across model families, but the magnitude depends on tokenizer-specific format compliance. We characterize when and why each formulation succeeds.\"\n48\t- Path B (CWM ≥ CLS on DeepSeek): \"No single formulation dominates: CLS wins on Qwen (tokenizer-induced CWM collapse) while CWM matches/exceeds CLS on DeepSeek. The formulation design space is model-contingent, and we provide the empirical characterization needed to navigate it.\"\n49\t\n50\t## Venue Fit Risks\n51\t\n52\t1. **Novelty concern (Dream consensus)**: \"Classification > generation when outputs are complex\" is predictable. Mitigation: novelty is in (a) empirical demonstration that it's tokenizer-specific not formulation-inherent, (b) error complementarity analysis, (c) systematic controlled comparison methodology. Frame as \"empirical characterization with actionable insights\" not \"method contribution.\"\n53\t\n54\t2. **EMNLP vs SE venue**: Mitigation: (a) process vs outcome supervision parallel (Lightman et al., 2023; Uesato et al., 2022), (b) general NLP methodology insight about classification vs generation.\n55\t\n56\t3. **CWM comparison fairness**: Original CWM is 32B with mid-training on execution traces; we compare 4-8B LoRA. Mitigation: explicitly scope as \"same-size LoRA comparison\" and discuss that generation may need more capacity. This limitation actually strengthens the practical guidance: \"at practical deployment sizes (4-8B), classification is the safer choice.\"\n57\t\n58\t4. **Re-repair null result**: Downstream utility unvalidated. Mitigation: frame efficiency + diagnostic granularity as the practical benefit, not re-repair. Re-repair is future work.\n59\t\n60\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Mitigations (updated per Dream)\n61\t\n62\t| Concern | Mitigation |\n63\t|---------|------------|\n64\t| \"Limited novelty — obvious that classification is easier\" | Func-level null result proves it's NOT trivially easier; advantage is complexity+tokenizer-dependent. Strongest novelty: CWM failure is tokenizer-specific |\n65\t| \"CWM comparison unfair (4-8B LoRA vs 32B full)\" | Explicitly scope as same-size comparison; discuss scale as limitation; practical guidance framing |\n66\t| \"Single-seed repo-level\" | Multi-seed replication (2 seeds × 2 formulations × DeepSeek, running) |\n67\t| \"Cross-model validation incomplete\" | DeepSeek repo experiments (running); whichever direction supports the design space characterization |\n68\t| \"No downstream utility\" | Efficiency benefit (6-9x) is practical; re-repair honestly framed as future work |\n69\t| \"Claims outrun evidence\" | D116: remove all advocacy language; report what evidence supports, nothing more |\n70\t| \"Process vs outcome supervision parallel\" | Add Lightman et al. 2023, Uesato et al. 2022 to Related Work |\n71\t\n72\t## Related Work Additions Needed (Dream feedback)\n73\t- Process vs outcome supervision: Lightman et al. 2023, Uesato et al. 2022 (CLS vs TRAJ parallel)\n74\t- CodeRL, AlphaCode, Reflexion (verification-in-the-loop repair)\n75\t- Predictive mutation testing literature (per-test binary classification precedent)\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ef0538b-31f1-4cc1-8254-5906cf02a00b","uuid":"93f0a39e-c136-42b4-b58f-d03adccf1cfb","timestamp":"2026-05-17T03:57:28.393Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — exec-sim-repair-verifier\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25, 2026)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Extended fallback: ICSE 2027 (Jun 23 abstract, Jun 30 paper)\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (9 days remaining)\n\n## D116 Framing Pivot (2026-05-16)\n\n**前 framing**: \"CLS scales where generation fails\" (CLS advocacy)\n**新 framing**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\" (empirical characterization)\n\n核心转变：不再主张任何 formulation 普遍更好，而是 characterize 何时用什么。\n最强贡献重新定位：\n1. CWM 失败模式是 **tokenizer/model-specific** 而非 formulation-inherent（Qwen 93% unparseable vs DeepSeek 11.8%）\n2. Error complementarity: CLS/CWM 有系统性互补错误模式，oracle ensemble +2-3pp\n3. 复杂度是 formulation 选择的关键维度（func-level 等价，repo-level 分化）\n\n## Core Claims (revised per D116)\n\n1. **Formulation Equivalence at Low Complexity**: At function-level, CLS, CWM, and TRAJ achieve statistically equivalent accuracy across two model families (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2%). Formulation choice does not matter when execution outputs are short and structured.\n\n2. **Complexity-Dependent Divergence (Model-Contingent)**: At repo-level, formulation effectiveness depends on model/tokenizer properties:\n   - Qwen3: CLS dominates CWM by 19-26pp due to 54-93% unparseable rate (tokenizer-specific CWM collapse)\n   - DeepSeek: ⏳ **PENDING** — results determine narrative direction:\n     - If CLS > CWM: \"complexity-dependent advantage is consistent across model families\"\n     - If CWM ≥ CLS: \"advantage is model/tokenizer-contingent; CWM works when tokenizer supports structured generation\"\n   - Either outcome supports the design space characterization framing\n\n3. **Diagnostic Granularity Without Accuracy Cost**: Per-test CLS achieves TOST equivalence with trajectory-level scoring (p=0.007, ±2pp margin, Qwen3) while providing per-test failure localization. Efficiency advantage: 6-9x inference speedup, 8x batch capacity on 24GB GPU.\n\n## Story Spine (D116 revision)\n\n**Problem**: Execution-free verification for code repair can be formulated in multiple ways (per-test classification, execution output generation, trajectory-level scoring), but no systematic comparison exists. Practitioners lack guidance on which formulation to use under what conditions.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Code World Models generate execution outputs but suffer from format compliance failures at scale. Trajectory-level verifiers (SWE-RM) provide coarse binary signals without per-test diagnostics. Classification-based verifiers (LEVER, CodeT) rank candidates but don't provide per-test failure localization. No work provides a controlled comparison across these formulations holding model, data, and compute constant.\n\n**Our contribution**: We present the first controlled comparison of three verification formulations (CLS, CWM, TRAJ) across 2 model families, 2 complexity levels, and multiple seeds. Key findings:\n1. Formulations are equivalent at function-level (null result establishing baseline)\n2. Formulations diverge at repo-level in a model-contingent manner\n3. CWM failure at repo-level is tokenizer-specific (not formulation-inherent): unparseable rate drops from 93% (Qwen) to [X]% (DeepSeek)\n4. CLS and CWM exhibit complementary error patterns (oracle ensemble: 90.63%)\n5. Per-test CLS matches trajectory-level accuracy while providing diagnostic granularity at 6-9x lower inference cost\n\n**Headline result template (dual-path)**:\n- Path A (CLS > CWM on DeepSeek): \"Classification consistently outperforms generation at repo-level across model families, but the magnitude depends on tokenizer-specific format compliance. We characterize when and why each formulation succeeds.\"\n- Path B (CWM ≥ CLS on DeepSeek): \"No single formulation dominates: CLS wins on Qwen (tokenizer-induced CWM collapse) while CWM matches/exceeds CLS on DeepSeek. The formulation design space is model-contingent, and we provide the empirical characterization needed to navigate it.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n1. **Novelty concern (Dream consensus)**: \"Classification > generation when outputs are complex\" is predictable. Mitigation: novelty is in (a) empirical demonstration that it's tokenizer-specific not formulation-inherent, (b) error complementarity analysis, (c) systematic controlled comparison methodology. Frame as \"empirical characterization with actionable insights\" not \"method contribution.\"\n\n2. **EMNLP vs SE venue**: Mitigation: (a) process vs outcome supervision parallel (Lightman et al., 2023; Uesato et al., 2022), (b) general NLP methodology insight about classification vs generation.\n\n3. **CWM comparison fairness**: Original CWM is 32B with mid-training on execution traces; we compare 4-8B LoRA. Mitigation: explicitly scope as \"same-size LoRA comparison\" and discuss that generation may need more capacity. This limitation actually strengthens the practical guidance: \"at practical deployment sizes (4-8B), classification is the safer choice.\"\n\n4. **Re-repair null result**: Downstream utility unvalidated. Mitigation: frame efficiency + diagnostic granularity as the practical benefit, not re-repair. Re-repair is future work.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Mitigations (updated per Dream)\n\n| Concern | Mitigation |\n|---------|------------|\n| \"Limited novelty — obvious that classification is easier\" | Func-level null result proves it's NOT trivially easier; advantage is complexity+tokenizer-dependent. Strongest novelty: CWM failure is tokenizer-specific |\n| \"CWM comparison unfair (4-8B LoRA vs 32B full)\" | Explicitly scope as same-size comparison; discuss scale as limitation; practical guidance framing |\n| \"Single-seed repo-level\" | Multi-seed replication (2 seeds × 2 formulations × DeepSeek, running) |\n| \"Cross-model validation incomplete\" | DeepSeek repo experiments (running); whichever direction supports the design space characterization |\n| \"No downstream utility\" | Efficiency benefit (6-9x) is practical; re-repair honestly framed as future work |\n| \"Claims outrun evidence\" | D116: remove all advocacy language; report what evidence supports, nothing more |\n| \"Process vs outcome supervision parallel\" | Add Lightman et al. 2023, Uesato et al. 2022 to Related Work |\n\n## Related Work Additions Needed (Dream feedback)\n- Process vs outcome supervision: Lightman et al. 2023, Uesato et al. 2022 (CLS vs TRAJ parallel)\n- CodeRL, AlphaCode, Reflexion (verification-in-the-loop repair)\n- Predictive mutation testing literature (per-test binary classification precedent)\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}}}
